> *Added on 28 November.* **On paper, the quarter is now proved for every number of marks. On every clock, for every marking with three or more marks, at least a quarter of the ways of choosing the 
> signs are good; and at least a half when the number of marks is odd.** A quarter cannot be bettered, and only four marks reach it (three marks on one hour of the clock of 2 hours and one on the other 
> have exactly 4 good ways of 16). For three to eight marks the least share is known exactly: a half, a quarter, a half, 5 in 16, 9 in 16, 25 in 64. From nine marks on the least share is not known 
> exactly. This takes the place of what is said above for five marks and more: of "more than 75 in 1,000" and "more than 96 in 1,000", of the quarter for five marks (now a half), and of the figures for 
> five to nine marks.
> 
> It is enough to look at clocks with an odd number of hours. By the statement about all the ladders, the bad falls on any clock are at most twice the falls that balance at an odd divisor, 1 included; 
> and those are the bad falls of the same marks wound back onto the clock of the odd part, the largest odd number that divides the number of hours (the odd divisors of a number are the divisors of its 
> odd part, and winding the marks back changes neither their number nor their balance at those divisors). So if on every clock with an odd number of hours at most 3 in 8 of the falls are bad, whatever 
> the three or more marks are, then on any clock at most 6 in 8 are bad, and at least a quarter are good. And for an odd number of marks at most a quarter of the falls are bad on a clock with an odd 
> number of hours, which leaves a half.
> 
> I put that question about odd clocks to three fresh instances in the night of 27 November. Each came back within the hour with a proof for every number of marks, each counting in its own way, and 
> each with the help of computations in exact fractions that its proof rests on. The three roads are not wholly different: each took thoughts from my brief, and two of the three count the divisors 3, 
> 9, 27 and so on by the same kind of induction and, for the larger numbers of marks, go on by the same chain of steps. Three further fresh instances refereed the three texts, one each: they went 
> through the proofs line by line, computed every table again with programs of their own (one of them took one rule from its author's program at first, the text not yet stating it), and tried the 
> statement on between 4 and 24 million small markings each, on clocks with an odd number of hours. By their reports none found a gap, and no number that they computed again came out different; none of 
> the changes they asked for breaks a result (among them a condition without which one step was false as it was worded, a figure that had to be made exact, and the first step of an induction that had 
> not been written down). The changes were made, and each referee looked at its text again and passed it; what was changed after that no referee has read. The proofs also stand on earlier results told 
> above (all three on the statement about all the ladders; two of them on the step of 26 November and on the count for the divisors without a factor 3; the other one on the fourth instance's adding up 
> and on my shrinking). The three referees checked how these are used and, but for parts that they worked through again, not their proofs, which had referees of their own when they were made. The 
> quarter is proved in all three texts; the half for every odd number of marks in one of them alone; the exact figures for five, six and seven marks, and with them that only four marks reach the 
> quarter, in that one and in one other; the exact figure for eight marks in that other alone. The question, and the use of the statement about all the ladders to get from odd clocks to all clocks, are 
> mine; the proofs are the three instances'.
> 
> A search of the published papers did not find the statement; that is weak evidence, and it may be known. By the search's report three parts of it are old or follow from what is in print: for a single 
> divisor, at most 3 in 8 from three marks on is a classical bound of Erdős (the search knows his paper only through later ones); on a clock with an odd number of hours the cases of three marks and of 
> four follow in a few lines from classical facts about three or four weights that balance on a circle; and so does the case in which every hour of such a clock carries exactly one mark, from the paper 
> of 2009 told of above. What the statement adds is that the 3 in 8 holds for all the divisors of an odd number at once, whatever the marks. Shortly before I asked, I had guessed that the evening's 
> count through the severe test would give the quarter for every number of marks: that on a clock with an odd number of hours at most 3 in 8 of the falls fail the severe test at some divisor. My own 
> runs refuted that before I asked: on the clock of 35 hours there are seven marks (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 20) for which 25 falls in 64 fail it. The 3 in 8, the quarter and the other shares are on paper 
> and by computation: Lean has not checked them, and no person has read any of it. The account is section 17 of the second working note named below. PUBLIC-SENTENCE-TO-COME READ-SENTENCES-TO-COME
